𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟱𝟵/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — Kubernetes Pod Stuck in ContainerCreating? 🔍

Imagine this scenario:

You deploy a new application to Kubernetes. The Pod is created successfully, but its status remains "ContainerCreating" for several minutes.

The application is not starting, and there are no application logs available yet.

How do you troubleshoot it?

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: Describe the Pod

Start by checking the Pod events:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look at the Events section at the bottom. It often reveals the actual reason.

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: Check the common causes

A Pod can remain in "ContainerCreating" because of:

• Volume mount or PVC attachment issues
• Image pulling delays
• CNI network plugin failures
• Container runtime problems
• Secret or ConfigMap mount issues
• Node-level resource or filesystem problems

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: Verify PVC and volume mounts

kubectl get pvc -n <namespace>
kubectl describe pvc <pvc-name> -n <namespace>

Check whether the PVC is "Bound" and whether the volume can be mounted.

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: Check the image

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for image pull errors in the Events section.

Verify the image name, tag, registry access, and image pull secrets.

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: Check the worker node

kubectl get pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> -o wide
kubectl describe node <node-name>

Check node conditions, disk pressure, and whether the container runtime or CNI is healthy.

𝗦𝘀𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: Check events

kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

Use the latest events to identify which initialization step is failing.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

"ContainerCreating" does not automatically mean the application has an issue.

Kubernetes may still be preparing the container environment — networking, storage, image, and runtime setup must succeed before the container starts.

Always investigate Pod events before making changes.
